# Identity of the kernel we build.
#
# IDENTITY switches between two builds of the same source:
#
#   repro  - build it as "Arkael", the name the working image on the phone uses.
#            Vermagic must come out identical. This is how we prove the pipeline
#            reproduces a kernel we know boots before we change anything.
#
#   own    - build it as "Unbothered". Same tree, same config, different name,
#            therefore a different vermagic. Only run this after `repro` boots.
#
# Override from the environment:  IDENTITY=repro ./scripts/10-build.sh

IDENTITY="${IDENTITY:-own}"

# --- our identity ----------------------------------------------------------
KERNEL_NAME="Unbothered"
# Shows up in uname -a as "<user>@<host>". Change these to your own handle.
KERNEL_USER="unbothered"
KERNEL_HOST="github-actions"

# --- what the working image was built as (do not edit) ---------------------
REPRO_NAME="Arkael"
REPRO_USER="arqzey"
REPRO_HOST="Arch-linux"

# --- build options ---------------------------------------------------------
# vortexcore | arkael
GOVERNOR_CHOICE="${GOVERNOR_CHOICE:-vortexcore}"

# --- root flavour ----------------------------------------------------------
# KernelSU-Next, for both identities.
#
# This was wrong at first, and the config diff is what caught it. The working
# image carries CONFIG_KSU_SAMSUNG_KDP/RKP/DEFEX, CONFIG_KSU_CONTAINER_COMPAT and
# CONFIG_KSU_THRONE_TRACKER_ALWAYS_THREADED, and carries none of BakaSU's
# MULTI_MANAGER/TOOLKIT/FULL_NAME options. That set is pershoot/KernelSU-Next
# exactly, not ReSukiSU/BakaSU (which is what build.sh installs for KSU=sukisu).
#
# So the phone runs KernelSU-Next, and "reproduce it" means KSU=yes.
#
#   yes = KernelSU-Next + SUSFS   (what the phone runs, and what we ship)
#   no  = vanilla, no root
#   sukisu = ReSukiSU/BakaSU. Not used: it produces a different CONFIG_KSU block,
#            which is precisely how we know the phone is not running it.
KSU="${KSU:-yes}"
KSU_SUSFS="${KSU_SUSFS:-true}"

# --- the two reference kernels, both of which boot on marble -----------------
#
#   marble-working.config   Arkael  5.10.272   KernelSU-Next + SUSFS  thin LTO
#   templar-working.config  Templar 5.10.271   KernelSU-Next + SUSFS  full LTO
#
# marble is the primary reference: it is the kernel this phone is running, so
# matching it is the strongest statement available. templar is the second
# booting sample, used to catch a root block that matches neither.
REF_CONFIG="marble-working.config"
KSU_REF_CONFIG="marble-working.config"
KSU_REF_CONFIG_2="templar-working.config"
